# Onboarding — getting this repo deploying to the cluster

Two roles are involved: the **tenant** (you, building the app) and the
**operator** (whoever runs the cluster). The tenant fills in workloads; the
operator adds one wiring file to the cluster repo. The cluster repo's
`kubernetes/clusters/<cluster>/tenants/README.md`, rendered from
weisssrv-cluster-template, is canonical for the shape of that file and for why
each object is there. This page renders this tenant's values into that shape.

Everything below is already rendered with this repo's answers: the slug is
`{{ app_slug }}`, the namespace is `{{ app_namespace }}` and the secret backend
is `{{ secrets_backend }}`.

---

## Tenant checklist

1. **Set the image** in `kubernetes/flux/deployment.yaml`. The shipped value is a
   **placeholder no registry has**
   (`{{ registry_host }}/{{ git_namespace | lower }}/{{ app_slug }}:REPLACE-ME`) — leave
   it and the first reconcile `ImagePullBackOff`s.
{%- if enable_image_build and ci_shape != 'none' %}
   CI builds the repo-root `Dockerfile` and pushes `:<short-sha>`, so that tag
   is the usual first edit.
{%- if ci_shape == 'gitlab_selfhosted' %}
   The build job runs only on a runner tagged `{{ privileged_runner_tag }}`,
   which the operator provides in O2e. Until it exists, the job stays Pending.
{%- endif %}
{%- if ci_shape == 'github' and registry_host != 'ghcr.io' %}
   The vendored build workflow publishes to `ghcr.io/<owner>/<repo>` from its
   own literals, so it does NOT push to `{{ registry_host }}`. Point `image:`
   at the GHCR path, or change the workflow's login step and its
   `REGISTRY`/`IMAGE_NAME`.
{%- endif %}
{%- if ci_shape == 'gitlab_selfhosted' and not (registry_host.split(':')[0] == git_host or registry_host.split(':')[0].endswith('.' ~ git_host)) %}
   The build job pushes `$CI_REGISTRY_IMAGE:<short-sha>` — this project's GitLab
   registry, not `{{ registry_host }}`. Point `image:` at the project registry,
   or pass `registry:`, `login_registry:`, `login_user:` and `login_password:`
   to the `docker-build` include.
{%- endif %}
{%- elif enable_image_build %}
   This repo has no pipeline: `task build` builds the repo-root `Dockerfile`
   locally and you push the tag yourself, then point `image:` at it.
{%- else %}
   This repo builds nothing, so point it at the upstream image you run.
{%- endif %}
{%- if enable_image_build and secrets_backend != 'none' and enable_registry_pull_secret %}
   The registry is private, so the namespace also needs a pull credential —
   step O2b below.
{%- elif enable_image_build and secrets_backend != 'none' %}
   There is **no** O2b step below: `enable_registry_pull_secret` is off, so
   nothing gives the kubelet a credential. If the registry turns out to be
   private — both a GitLab project registry and a GHCR package are, by default,
   and a GHCR package's visibility does not follow its source repository's —
   the fix is `copier update --data enable_registry_pull_secret=true`, or make
   the package public.
{%- elif enable_image_build %}
   There is **no** O2b step below: the pull credential is itself an
   ExternalSecret, so a private registry needs a `secrets_backend` first. Make
   the package public, or add a backend and the credential in one
   `copier update`.
{%- elif secrets_backend != 'none' and enable_registry_pull_secret %}
   The upstream registry is private, so the namespace still needs a pull
   credential — step O2b below.
{%- endif %}
2. **Check the hostnames.**
{%- if enable_public_ingress %} Public `{{ app_slug }}.{{ external_domain }}` is
   ready in `ingressroute.yaml` and needs no operator action.
{%- else %} This app publishes no public hostname. `enable_public_ingress: true`
   renders the public route and its certificate together.
{%- endif %}
{%- if enable_internal_ingress %}
   The internal `{{ app_slug }}.{{ internal_domain }}` route ships too, and it
   DOES need one: step O5.
{%- else %}
   For a LAN/tailnet hostname as well, re-run `copier update` with
   `enable_internal_ingress: true` (it renders the route and its certificate
   together) and ask the operator for step O5.
{%- endif %}
{%- if secrets_backend == 'none' %}
3. **Secrets.** This repo has none. If the app grows one, add an
   `ExternalSecret` referencing the store the operator provisions in step O3 —
   never a value in git.
{%- else %}
3. **Wire the secrets** in `kubernetes/flux/externalsecret.yaml`. It references
   the `ClusterSecretStore` `{{ secrets_backend }}-{{ app_slug }}`, which is what
   the operator creates in step O3 — the two names must match.
{%- if secrets_backend == 'onepassword' %}
   The item must carry a field named exactly as `property` spells it (`api-key`
   as shipped).
{%- else %}
   The project must carry a masked CI/CD variable named exactly as
   `remoteRef.key` spells it (`APP_API_KEY` as shipped); GitLab variable names
   allow only letters, digits and `_`.
{%- endif %}
   The Deployment's env binding is not `optional:`, so until the ExternalSecret
   syncs, the pod stays in `CreateContainerConfigError`.
{%- endif %}
{%- if enable_servicemonitor %}
4. **Point observability** at the app's real metrics endpoint in
   `servicemonitor.yaml`, and keep `networkpolicy.yaml`'s scrape allow on the
   same port. A ServiceMonitor pointed at an endpoint that 404s scrapes
   `up == 0` and raises a TargetDown alert in the OPERATOR's Alertmanager.
{%- else %}
4. **Metrics are off.** When the app exposes `/metrics`, re-run `copier update`
   with `enable_servicemonitor: true` — it renders the ServiceMonitor and the
   matching scrape NetworkPolicy together.
{%- endif %}
5. `task lint`, commit on a branch, open a {{ change_request }}.
{%- if ci_shape == 'none' %}
   There is no pipeline here, so `task lint` and the pre-commit hooks are the
   whole gate.
{%- elif ci_shape == 'github' %}
   CI must be green. The workflows are byte-identical library copies, so they
   cannot take an answer: set repository variable `K8S_VERSION` to
   `{{ k8s_version }}` under Settings > Secrets and variables > Actions. Until
   it is set, `flux-lint` warns and validates the manifests against the
   library's own fallback minor.
{%- else %}
   CI must be green.
{%- endif %}
{%- if forge == 'gitlab' %}
   Optional: create the project labels you want (Settings > Labels), then add a
   `/label ~<name>` line to the templates under `.gitlab/` to tag new issues and
   {{ change_request }}s automatically.
{%- endif %}
6. **Request wiring** from the operator: hand them this page. They need the slug
   (`{{ app_slug }}`), the namespace (`{{ app_namespace }}`), the secret backend
   (`{{ secrets_backend }}`), this repo's URL, whether an internal hostname is
   needed, whether the repo is private (O2c){% if secrets_backend != 'none' and enable_registry_pull_secret %}, the registry pull credential (O2b){% endif %}{% if ci_shape == 'gitlab_selfhosted' %}, library job-token access (O2d){% endif %}{% if ci_shape == 'gitlab_selfhosted' and enable_image_build %}, the `{{ privileged_runner_tag }}` build runner (O2e){% endif %}{% if enable_sso %} and the SSO objects (O8){% endif %}.
   Replace the `:REPLACE-ME` image tag first: the placeholder leaves the
   Deployment at zero available replicas and pages the operator.
7. After the operator merges the wiring, merge your `main`. Verify with
   `task flux:status` and `kubectl get pods -n {{ app_namespace }}`, using the
   read-only kubeconfig the operator hands you in O10.

---

## Operator checklist (performed in the cluster repo)

**Before the first tenant.** A cluster that has never onboarded a tenant may
still carry platform settings that were safe only while every route and every
ExternalSecret was operator-authored. Cross-namespace Traefik routing and the
Flux agent's RBAC are the usual two. Work the cluster repo's pre-onboarding
checklist first; its lint can fail on the first tenant entry until that is done.
The cluster-side objects this repo binds to are listed in `ARCHITECTURE.md`
under Platform contract. Merge the wiring only once the tenant repo carries a
real image tag: the shipped `:REPLACE-ME` placeholder holds the Deployment at
zero available replicas and `AppDown` (critical) fires in your Alertmanager ten
minutes later.
{%- if secrets_backend != 'none' %}

A backend item missing the field the `ExternalSecret` names pages you the same
way: the pod's env binding is not `optional:`, so it never starts. Merge the
wiring once the item carries that field.
{%- endif %}

### O1 — Prepare the secret backend
{% if secrets_backend == 'onepassword' %}
This tenant gets its own vault, `{{ onepassword_vault }}`, and a Connect token
scoped to that vault alone. Never put its items in the cluster's own vault. A
store's `conditions` limit which namespace may use it, not which items it reads.
A tenant token covering the cluster vault lets this namespace read every
platform credential.

Look the vault up first:

```bash
op vault get "{{ onepassword_vault }}"
```

Create it only when that reports the vault does not exist. Stop on any other
error and fix it first. 1Password allows two vaults with one name, so a failed
lookup followed by a create leaves the grant and the items in different vaults.

Then grant the vault to the cluster's shared Connect server and mint the
tenant's token. `op connect server list` prints the server ID. `<CLUSTER>` is
the cluster's name, which keeps token names unique on a Connect server that
several clusters share. The grant adds the vault to the running server, so
nothing restarts.

```bash
op vault create "{{ onepassword_vault }}"   # only when the lookup found none
op connect vault grant --server <SERVER_ID> --vault "{{ onepassword_vault }}"
op connect token create <CLUSTER>-{{ app_slug }}-eso --server <SERVER_ID> --vaults "{{ onepassword_vault }}"
```

Never hand this tenant the platform token. Create these items in the tenant
vault with `op item create --vault "{{ onepassword_vault }}"`:

- `"{{ app_slug }}: {{ secret_item }}"` with field `api-key`.
{%- if enable_registry_pull_secret %}
- `"{{ app_slug }}: Registry Pull"` with fields `username` and `token` (O2b).
{%- endif %}
{%- if database == 'postgres' %}
- `"{{ app_slug }}: {{ db_roles_item }}"` with fields `migrate-password`,
  `app-password` and `ro-password` (O6b).
- `"{{ app_slug }}: {{ db_backup_item }}"` with fields `access-key-id` and
  `secret-access-key` (O6b).
{%- endif %}
{%- elif secrets_backend == 'gitlab' %}
Have the tenant create a project access token with the **Maintainer** role and
the `api` scope. It belongs on the project that holds this repo's CI/CD
variables, on the GitLab instance at `{{ gitlab_api_url }}`. That project's
numeric ID is the `projectID` in the store below. Reading project CI/CD
variables is a Maintainer-level operation, and the ESO GitLab provider requires
`api`, not `read_api`.

That token can also rewrite the project's code, variables and settings, and it
sits in the cluster as a Secret. Keep the variables in a dedicated project that
holds nothing else, so the token reaches nothing else. Then set the masked
CI/CD variables this repo's ExternalSecret reads on that project.
{%- else %}
Nothing to prepare — this tenant declares no secret backend. Skip O1, O2 and the
`ClusterSecretStore` in O3.
{%- endif %}
{%- if secrets_backend != 'none' %}

### O2 — Bootstrap the namespace secret

One-time, not Flux-managed, and re-runnable for a rotated token:

```bash
kubectl create namespace {{ app_namespace }} --dry-run=client -o yaml | kubectl apply -f -
{%- if secrets_backend == 'onepassword' %}
kubectl -n {{ app_namespace }} create secret generic onepassword-connect-token \
  --from-literal=token=<CONNECT_TOKEN> --dry-run=client -o yaml | kubectl apply -f -
{%- else %}
kubectl -n {{ app_namespace }} create secret generic gitlab-api-token \
  --from-literal=token=<PROJECT_ACCESS_TOKEN> --dry-run=client -o yaml | kubectl apply -f -
{%- endif %}
```

The pod reads the synced value into its environment at container start, so a
rotation also needs
`kubectl delete pod -n {{ app_namespace }} -l app.kubernetes.io/name={{ app_slug }}`.
A `rollout restart` is not a substitute: it writes a pod-template annotation the
cluster's kustomize-controller reverts on its next reconcile. The
`ExternalSecret` reports Ready either way.

The Secret has no controller owner, so the cluster's unmanaged-secrets gate
flags it. In the same change as the wiring file, add this entry to the
`ALLOWLIST` in the cluster repo's `check-unmanaged-secrets.py`:

```python
    "{{ app_namespace }}/{{ 'onepassword-connect-token' if secrets_backend == 'onepassword' else 'gitlab-api-token' }}": "tenant ESO bootstrap",
```
{%- endif %}
{%- if secrets_backend != 'none' and enable_registry_pull_secret %}

### O2b — Registry pull credential

{% if registry_pull_host == 'ghcr.io' or registry_host == 'ghcr.io' -%}
A GHCR package is private by default and its visibility does **not** follow its
source repository's, so the kubelet cannot pull this tenant's image without a
credential.

1. Create a personal access token carrying the `read:packages` scope on an
   account that can read the package — GitHub's documented credential for
   `ghcr.io` is a **classic** token — and confirm that account (or the tenant
   repo) is granted access to the package itself, which is a separate grant
   from the source repository's.
2. Store the token and the **username it belongs to** (the GitHub account name)
   in the tenant's secret backend under the fields
   `kubernetes/flux/externalsecret-registry.yaml` reads. The username is half
   of what `docker login ghcr.io` takes, so it must be stored, not assumed.
{%- elif forge == 'gitlab' and (registry_host.split(':')[0] == git_host or registry_host.split(':')[0].endswith('.' ~ git_host)) -%}
A project registry is private by default, so the kubelet cannot pull this
tenant's image without a credential.

1. In the **tenant** project, create a deploy token scoped `read_registry`. The
   token *name* is not a secret; the value is.
2. Store the token and its **username** in the tenant's secret backend under the
   fields `kubernetes/flux/externalsecret-registry.yaml` reads — the forge
   generates the username (e.g. `gitlab+deploy-token-N`), so it must be stored,
   not assumed.
{%- else -%}
A private registry means the kubelet cannot pull this tenant's image without a
credential.

1. On `{{ registry_host }}`, create a read-only credential for this image — a
   robot account, deploy token or pull-scoped token, whatever the registry
   calls it.
2. Store the token and the **username it belongs to** in the tenant's secret
   backend under the fields
   `kubernetes/flux/externalsecret-registry.yaml` reads. The username is half of
   what `docker login` takes, so it must be stored, not assumed.
{%- endif %}

{% if registry_pull_host != registry_host -%}
The image reference names `{{ registry_host }}`, and `{{ registry_pull_host }}`
is keyed into the same credential so the pull still authenticates on a cluster
whose nodes resolve that name to an internal registry. The kubelet matches a
{%- else -%}
The image reference and the credential both name `{{ registry_host }}`.
The kubelet matches a
{%- endif %}
pull secret against the literal host in the image reference, so if your nodes
must pull a different hostname, edit `image:` in
`kubernetes/flux/deployment.yaml` — nothing rewrites it. Making the {% if registry_pull_host == 'ghcr.io' or registry_host == 'ghcr.io' %}package{% else %}registry{% endif %}
repository public avoids this step entirely.
{%- endif %}

### O2c — Git credential

A public repository needs no credential: delete the `secretRef` from the
`GitRepository` in O3 and skip the rest of this step.

Flux clones `https://{{ git_host }}/{{ git_namespace }}/{{ app_slug }}` with the
Secret the wiring's `GitRepository` names in `secretRef`. Create it in
`flux-system`, beside the `GitRepository`, from a read-only credential.
{%- if forge == 'gitlab' %}
On GitLab that is a deploy token or project access token scoped to
`read_repository`.
{%- elif forge == 'github' %}
On GitHub that is a fine-grained token with read-only Contents access.
{%- endif %}
Rerun the same command to rotate it.

```bash
flux create secret git {{ app_slug }}-git-auth \
  --namespace=flux-system \
  --url=https://{{ git_host }}/{{ git_namespace }}/{{ app_slug }} \
  --username=<TOKEN_USERNAME> \
  --password=<TOKEN>
```

The Secret is bootstrap state with no controller owner, so add
`"flux-system/{{ app_slug }}-git-auth": "tenant git credential"` to the same
unmanaged-secrets `ALLOWLIST` in the wiring change. The cluster repo's tenants
README covers the deploy-key form under "Private tenant repositories".
{%- if ci_shape == 'gitlab_selfhosted' %}

### O2d — CI library access

The tenant pipeline's `ci-job-names-check` job clones `{{ lib_project }}` with
its `CI_JOB_TOKEN`. Unless that project is public, a maintainer of
`{{ lib_project }}` adds `{{ git_namespace }}/{{ app_slug }}` to its job-token
allowlist under Settings > CI/CD > Job token permissions. Without it, that job
fails on the clone and names the allowlist in its log. The pipeline's
`include:`s resolve with the permissions of whoever runs it, so each tenant
developer also needs read access to `{{ lib_project }}`.
{%- endif %}
{%- if ci_shape == 'gitlab_selfhosted' and enable_image_build %}

### O2e — Image build runner

The image build job carries the tag `{{ privileged_runner_tag }}` and runs as
root with Docker-in-Docker. Register a privileged runner with that tag to the
`{{ git_namespace }}` group, or to this project when that namespace is a user
rather than a group. Give the runner its own namespace, quota and egress policy.
Never give the tag to the cluster's own platform runner: every developer in this
repo would then run root on it. The cluster repo's `gitlab-runner-privileged`
README describes this second runner and the `runner_type` check that confirms
its registration scope. Without the runner, the build job stays Pending and
names no cause.
{%- endif %}

### O3 — Add the wiring file

The file binds two roles to the tenant's ServiceAccount. `admin` normally covers
external-secrets.io and cert-manager.io through their aggregated `*-edit` roles;
`tenant-crd-editor` covers those two as well, so a cluster missing an aggregated
role still applies them. A RoleBinding naming a ClusterRole the cluster does not
have applies without error and grants nothing, so create that role first on a
cluster with no tenants yet. A cluster repo generated from
weisssrv-cluster-template ships it at
`kubernetes/clusters/<cluster>/tenants/tenant-crd-editor.yaml`. On any other
cluster, create a ClusterRole granting edit on the API groups listed in
`ARCHITECTURE.md` § Platform contract.

The `tenant-routing-{{ app_namespace }}` ConfigMap grants the hostnames this
repo's routes and certificates name. The two bindings beside it hold the
namespace to that grant, and with no grant they refuse every route and
certificate. The Namespace, both caps, the grant and both bindings carry
`kustomize.toolkit.fluxcd.io/prune: disabled`. The cluster's
`check-tenant-wiring` and `check-tenant-traefik-isolation` gates fail when any
one of them is missing.

`kubernetes/clusters/<cluster>/tenants/{{ app_slug }}.yaml`:

```yaml
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Namespace
metadata:
  name: {{ app_namespace }}
  annotations:
    # Never pruned: a pruned Namespace takes every tenant object and PVC with
    # it. Teardown is deliberate; the tenants README's opening section covers it.
    kustomize.toolkit.fluxcd.io/prune: disabled
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/managed-by: flux
    fluxcd.io/tenant: {{ app_slug }}
    # Pod Security Admission — baseline enforced, restricted advised.
    pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce: baseline
    pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn: restricted
    pod-security.kubernetes.io/audit: restricted
    # CRITICAL: no platform-store membership label here. It is what the
    # platform ClusterSecretStore admits, and it would hand this tenant every
    # item in the platform vault.{% if secrets_backend != 'none' %} This tenant gets its own store below.{% endif %}
---
# Cap the namespace: `admin` places no ceiling on what a tenant may request.
# `pods:` is the replica count plus the rollout surge, floored at 10;
# `limits.memory` must cover that many per-pod memory limits{% if enable_vpa %} at the VPA's maxAllowed{% endif %}.
apiVersion: v1
kind: ResourceQuota
metadata:
  name: {{ app_slug }}-quota
  namespace: {{ app_namespace }}
  annotations:
    # Kept with the Namespace, so an orphaned namespace stays capped.
    kustomize.toolkit.fluxcd.io/prune: disabled
spec:
  hard:
    pods: "{{ [replica_count | int + 2, 10] | max }}"
    requests.cpu: "5"
    requests.memory: 4Gi
    limits.memory: 12Gi
    # emptyDir and container writable layers draw on the node's disk, so this
    # bounds what the namespace may write there. check-tenant-wiring requires
    # both keys.
    requests.ephemeral-storage: 4Gi
    limits.ephemeral-storage: 8Gi
{%- if database == 'postgres' and secrets_backend == 'onepassword' %}
    # tenant-local does not enforce sizes on its shared volume, so the quota
    # caps what this namespace may request: the instance claim plus headroom.
    persistentvolumeclaims: "4"
    tenant-local.storageclass.storage.k8s.io/requests.storage: {{ (db_storage_size | replace('Gi', '') | int) * 2 }}Gi
{%- else %}
    # No claims: this tenant ships none. Raise both when the operator
    # provisions a volume.
    persistentvolumeclaims: "0"
    tenant-local.storageclass.storage.k8s.io/requests.storage: "0"
{%- endif %}
---
# Defaults and per-container ceilings, so a pod with no resources block cannot
# land unbounded inside the quota. A `max:` key with no matching `default:` key
# becomes the default limit, so CPU is left to the quota's requests.cpu.
apiVersion: v1
kind: LimitRange
metadata:
  name: {{ app_slug }}-limits
  namespace: {{ app_namespace }}
  annotations:
    # Kept with the Namespace, so an orphaned namespace stays capped.
    kustomize.toolkit.fluxcd.io/prune: disabled
spec:
  limits:
    - type: Container
      default:
        memory: 512Mi
        ephemeral-storage: 512Mi
      defaultRequest:
        cpu: 50m
        memory: 128Mi
        ephemeral-storage: 128Mi
      max:
        memory: 2Gi
        ephemeral-storage: 2Gi
{%- if secrets_backend == 'onepassword' %}
---
apiVersion: external-secrets.io/v1
kind: ClusterSecretStore
metadata:
  name: onepassword-{{ app_slug }}
spec:
  # A ClusterSecretStore is cluster-scoped: without `conditions`, ANY namespace
  # can reference it by name and read this store's vault. Scope every tenant
  # store to its own namespace.
  conditions:
    - namespaces:
        - {{ app_namespace }}
  provider:
    onepassword:
      # The cluster's 1Password Connect Service; change it if yours differs.
      # Plain HTTP: the token and every value cross the pod network as sent,
      # so use https here when Connect serves TLS.
      connectHost: http://onepassword-connect.external-secrets.svc.cluster.local:8080
      # This tenant's own vault and no other: the token is scoped to it alone.
      vaults:
        "{{ onepassword_vault }}": 1
      auth:
        secretRef:
          connectTokenSecretRef:
            name: onepassword-connect-token
            namespace: {{ app_namespace }}
            key: token
{%- elif secrets_backend == 'gitlab' %}
---
apiVersion: external-secrets.io/v1
kind: ClusterSecretStore
metadata:
  name: gitlab-{{ app_slug }}
spec:
  # A ClusterSecretStore is cluster-scoped: without `conditions`, ANY namespace
  # can reference it by name and read this store's variables. Scope every tenant
  # store to its own namespace, for every provider.
  conditions:
    - namespaces:
        - {{ app_namespace }}
  provider:
    gitlab:
      # `url` is optional and DEFAULTS TO https://gitlab.com — omitting it sends
      # a self-hosted project token to the wrong instance, and the 401/404 that
      # follows names nothing in this file. It is the `gitlab_api_url` answer.
      url: {{ gitlab_api_url }}
      projectID: "<tenant project id>"
      auth:
        # Capital S: the GitLab provider is the one whose auth field is
        # `SecretRef` in the CRD. A lower-cased key is pruned as unknown,
        # leaving `auth` with no credential.
        SecretRef:
          accessToken:
            name: gitlab-api-token
            namespace: {{ app_namespace }}
            key: token
      # Variables scoped to a specific environment are invisible without this.
      environment: "*"
{%- endif %}
---
apiVersion: source.toolkit.fluxcd.io/v1
kind: GitRepository
metadata:
  name: {{ app_slug }}
  namespace: flux-system
spec:
  interval: 1m
  url: https://{{ git_host }}/{{ git_namespace }}/{{ app_slug }}
  ref:
    branch: main
  # A read-only credential created by hand with `flux create secret git`
  # ("Private tenant repositories" in the tenants README). Delete this for a
  # public repository.
  secretRef:
    name: {{ app_slug }}-git-auth
---
# The hostnames this tenant may route and certify, one per line. It lives in
# flux-system so the tenant cannot edit its own grant.
apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: tenant-routing-{{ app_namespace }}
  namespace: flux-system
  annotations:
    # The Namespace outlives the wiring, so admission for it must too.
    kustomize.toolkit.fluxcd.io/prune: disabled
data:
  hosts: |
{%- if enable_public_ingress %}
    {{ app_slug }}.{{ external_domain }}
{%- endif %}
{%- if enable_internal_ingress %}
    {{ app_slug }}.{{ internal_domain }}
{%- endif %}
---
# Holds this namespace's IngressRoutes and Ingresses to the grant above, and
# refuses them all if the grant is missing.
apiVersion: admissionregistration.k8s.io/v1
kind: ValidatingAdmissionPolicyBinding
metadata:
  name: tenant-traefik-hostnames-{{ app_namespace }}
  annotations:
    # The Namespace outlives the wiring, so admission for it must too.
    kustomize.toolkit.fluxcd.io/prune: disabled
spec:
  policyName: tenant-traefik-hostnames
  validationActions: [Deny]
  paramRef:
    name: tenant-routing-{{ app_namespace }}
    namespace: flux-system
    parameterNotFoundAction: Deny
  matchResources:
    namespaceSelector:
      matchLabels:
        kubernetes.io/metadata.name: {{ app_namespace }}
---
# Holds this namespace's cert-manager Certificates to the same grant.
apiVersion: admissionregistration.k8s.io/v1
kind: ValidatingAdmissionPolicyBinding
metadata:
  name: tenant-certificates-{{ app_namespace }}
  annotations:
    # The Namespace outlives the wiring, so admission for it must too.
    kustomize.toolkit.fluxcd.io/prune: disabled
spec:
  policyName: tenant-certificates
  validationActions: [Deny]
  paramRef:
    name: tenant-routing-{{ app_namespace }}
    namespace: flux-system
    parameterNotFoundAction: Deny
  matchResources:
    namespaceSelector:
      matchLabels:
        kubernetes.io/metadata.name: {{ app_namespace }}
---
# A namespace-scoped ServiceAccount so kustomize-controller does NOT apply
# tenant manifests with its own cluster-admin. The SA lives in flux-system; the
# RoleBindings grant admin only in the tenant namespace.
apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: {{ app_slug }}-flux
  namespace: flux-system
---
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: RoleBinding
metadata:
  name: {{ app_slug }}-flux-admin
  namespace: {{ app_namespace }}
subjects:
  - kind: ServiceAccount
    name: {{ app_slug }}-flux
    namespace: flux-system
roleRef:
  kind: ClusterRole
  name: admin
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
---
# CRITICAL: `admin` does not aggregate traefik.io (IngressRoute) or
# monitoring.coreos.com (ServiceMonitor, PrometheusRule){% if enable_vpa %},
# nor autoscaling.k8s.io (VPA){% endif %}.
# Bind `tenant-crd-editor` as well, or the tenant Kustomization goes NotReady
# on the first IngressRoute.
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: RoleBinding
metadata:
  name: {{ app_slug }}-flux-crd-editor
  namespace: {{ app_namespace }}
subjects:
  - kind: ServiceAccount
    name: {{ app_slug }}-flux
    namespace: flux-system
roleRef:
  kind: ClusterRole
  name: tenant-crd-editor
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
---
# The identity behind the tenant's read-only kubeconfig.
apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: {{ app_slug }}-reader
  namespace: {{ app_namespace }}
---
# `view` reads the namespace's workloads, events and logs, never its Secrets.
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: RoleBinding
metadata:
  name: {{ app_slug }}-reader-view
  namespace: {{ app_namespace }}
subjects:
  - kind: ServiceAccount
    name: {{ app_slug }}-reader
    namespace: {{ app_namespace }}
roleRef:
  kind: ClusterRole
  name: view
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
---
# Read on this tenant's own Flux objects in flux-system, so
# `flux get kustomization {{ app_slug }}` reports its state. No patch, so
# `flux reconcile` is refused.
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role
metadata:
  name: {{ app_slug }}-reader
  namespace: flux-system
rules:
  - apiGroups: ["kustomize.toolkit.fluxcd.io"]
    resources: ["kustomizations"]
    resourceNames:
      - {{ app_slug }}
{%- if database == 'postgres' and secrets_backend == 'onepassword' %}
      - {{ app_slug }}-db
{%- endif %}
    verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
  - apiGroups: ["source.toolkit.fluxcd.io"]
    resources: ["gitrepositories"]
    resourceNames:
      - {{ app_slug }}
    verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
---
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: RoleBinding
metadata:
  name: {{ app_slug }}-reader
  namespace: flux-system
subjects:
  - kind: ServiceAccount
    name: {{ app_slug }}-reader
    namespace: {{ app_namespace }}
roleRef:
  kind: Role
  name: {{ app_slug }}-reader
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
---
apiVersion: kustomize.toolkit.fluxcd.io/v1
kind: Kustomization
metadata:
  name: {{ app_slug }}
  namespace: flux-system
spec:
  interval: 10m
  retryInterval: 1m
  timeout: 10m
  # Wait for the platform CRD chain so a fresh bootstrap does not apply this
  # tenant's CRs before the ESO, cert-manager and Traefik CRDs exist. Retarget
  # it at your own stage; docs/ARCHITECTURE.md, Platform contract.
  dependsOn:
    - name: infrastructure-configs
{%- if database == 'postgres' and secrets_backend == 'onepassword' %}
    - name: {{ app_slug }}-db
{%- endif %}
  serviceAccountName: {{ app_slug }}-flux
  sourceRef:
    kind: GitRepository
    name: {{ app_slug }}
  path: ./kubernetes/flux
  prune: true
  targetNamespace: {{ app_namespace }}
  wait: true
  # A cluster-template tenant-wiring gate requires this source. Nothing
  # under kubernetes/flux spells a variable, so it substitutes nothing.
  postBuild:
    substituteFrom:
      - kind: ConfigMap
        name: cluster-config
        optional: false
{%- if database == 'postgres' and secrets_backend == 'onepassword' %}
---
# The database tree reconciles first and on its own, so the cluster, its
# Secrets and its backups exist before any workload starts.
apiVersion: kustomize.toolkit.fluxcd.io/v1
kind: Kustomization
metadata:
  name: {{ app_slug }}-db
  namespace: flux-system
spec:
  interval: 10m
  retryInterval: 1m
  timeout: 10m
  dependsOn:
    - name: infrastructure-configs
  serviceAccountName: {{ app_slug }}-flux
  sourceRef:
    kind: GitRepository
    name: {{ app_slug }}
  path: ./kubernetes/db
  prune: true
  # Deleting this wiring must never half-dismantle a database: Orphan keeps
  # every object, so removal is the deliberate teardown, not a side effect.
  deletionPolicy: Orphan
  targetNamespace: {{ app_namespace }}
  wait: true
  # A cluster-template tenant-wiring gate requires this source. Nothing
  # under kubernetes/db spells a variable, so it substitutes nothing.
  postBuild:
    substituteFrom:
      - kind: ConfigMap
        name: cluster-config
        optional: false
{%- endif %}
```

Every Kustomization here carries `postBuild.substituteFrom` naming the
cluster's `cluster-config` ConfigMap, because the cluster-template tenant-wiring
gate requires it. Nothing substitutes unless a `${var}` appears under the path
it applies. Write a literal dollar-brace as `$${var}`: with strict substitution,
which kustomize-controller usually runs, one unresolvable `${var}` fails the
whole tenant reconcile. See `ARCHITECTURE.md`
§ Platform contract.

### O4 — Register it

Kustomize does not auto-discover, so add the file to the tenants
`kustomization.yaml`:

```yaml
resources:
  - tenant-crd-editor.yaml   # shipped by the cluster repo (see O3)
  - {{ app_slug }}.yaml      # add this line
```
{% if enable_internal_ingress %}
### O5 — Internal DNS

`{{ app_slug }}.{{ internal_domain }}` is not auto-provisioned — external-dns
manages the external zone only. Add the internal rewrite
(`{{ app_slug }}.{{ internal_domain }}` -> `{{ internal_vip }}`) to the cluster's
DNS inventory and run the DNS role.
{%- if enable_public_ingress %} Public
`{{ app_slug }}.{{ external_domain }}` needs nothing here.
{%- endif %}
{%- else %}
### O5 — Internal DNS

Not needed: this tenant publishes only the public hostname, which external-dns
provisions on its own.
{%- endif %}

### O6 — Persistent storage (only if the tenant needs a volume)

This template ships no PVC for the app by design.{% if database == 'postgres' and secrets_backend == 'onepassword' %} The database tree under `kubernetes/db` brings its own claims, covered in O6b.{% endif %} A tenant claim names
one of two things. One is the StorageClass `tenant-local`. The other is a
platform-written static PV named `{{ app_namespace }}-<purpose>`, pre-bound by
`claimRef` to a claim with `storageClassName: ""`. A cluster generated by
weisssrv-cluster-template refuses any other class or volume name at admission.
Hand the tenant the PVC name. The tenant then adds the volume and its mount to
`deployment.yaml`{% if enable_vpa %} and sets `vpa.yaml` `updateMode: "Off"`{% endif %}.
Raise the wiring quota's `persistentvolumeclaims` key in the same change, and
its `tenant-local` storage key for a `tenant-local` claim, or the claim is
refused.

The cluster repo's `kubernetes/clusters/<cluster>/tenants/README.md` covers
both forms under "Persistent storage" and "Claim classes". A volume pinned to
one node also needs the node affinity and toleration that node carries. Hand
the tenant both with the PV/PVC.

{% if not (database == 'postgres' and secrets_backend == 'onepassword') -%}
This app is stateless as generated. A volume provisioned here sits outside
every backup set until the operator adds it, and the operator owns its snapshot
and backup schedule.
{%- else -%}
The app's state lives in the CloudNativePG cluster under `kubernetes/db`; see
O6b for its backups and restore drill. A volume provisioned here sits outside
every backup set until the operator adds it, and the operator owns its snapshot
and backup schedule.
{%- endif %}

{% if database == 'postgres' and secrets_backend == 'onepassword' -%}
### O6b — Database prerequisites

Enabling `database: postgres` requires the app to serve `/readyz`, returning
200 only when its connection to `{{ app_slug }}-db-rw` succeeds: the handler
connects with the mounted `{{ app_slug }}-db-app` credentials
(`/var/run/secrets/db`) and `sslrootcert=/var/run/secrets/db-ca/ca.crt`, and
answers 503 when that fails.
{%- if enable_image_build %} The placeholder image (the `Dockerfile`'s
`python -m http.server` serving `/app`) does NOT serve `/readyz`, so the pod
stays NotReady until your app replaces it. That is intended: a Ready pod with no
database would be worse.
{%- endif %}

The database component needs, before the wiring merges:

- The cluster's CloudNativePG operator and Barman Cloud plugin, and
  `tenant-crd-editor` granting the `postgresql.cnpg.io` and
  `barmancloud.cnpg.io` kinds (weisssrv-cluster-template `postgres_operator`).
- The cluster's `tenant-local` StorageClass (weisssrv-cluster-template
  `tenant_storage`), which provisions the volume for
  `{{ app_slug }}-db-1` when the cluster first starts. The wiring file's
  ResourceQuota above already counts the claim.
- An NFS PersistentVolume `{{ app_namespace }}-pg-dump` pre-bound to
  `{{ app_namespace }}/{{ app_slug }}-pg-dump`, on a landing zone the off-site
  backup walks, exported with `all_squash` to uid 1000 and gid 2000.
- The dump registered with the backup-artifact freshness check of a cluster
  generated by weisssrv-cluster-template: the entry
  `{name: {{ app_slug }}, pattern: "{{ app_slug }}-*.sql.gz"}` in
  `nas_storage_backup_artifact_apps`, and the matching
  `or absent(backup_artifact_last_mtime_seconds{app="{{ app_slug }}"})` arm on
  `BackupArtifactStale`. The cluster's `check-backup-artifact-apps` gate
  requires both.
- The bucket `{{ db_backup_bucket }}` at `{{ db_backup_endpoint }}` with a key
  scoped to it alone, and lifecycle rules that keep deleted versions at least
  30 days, in 1Password item `"{{ app_slug }}: {{ db_backup_item }}"` (fields
  `access-key-id`, `secret-access-key`).
- 1Password item `"{{ app_slug }}: {{ db_roles_item }}"` with fields
  `migrate-password`, `app-password` and `ro-password`, each generated, never
  typed, from a letters, digits and symbols recipe without spaces (for example
  `op item create ... 'migrate-password[password]=<generated>'`).
- The cluster's CloudNativePG alert rules, and a restore drill (PITR into a
  scratch namespace) before the database holds data that matters.

The application reads its connection from files under `/var/run/secrets/db`
(host, port, dbname, user, password, sslmode, uri) with the CA at
`/var/run/secrets/db-ca/ca.crt`, and serves `/readyz` as described in
`kubernetes/flux/deployment.yaml`.

{% endif -%}
### O7 — Reachability probe (recommended for user-facing routes)

This repo's `PrometheusRule` covers replica availability only. The platform
alerts cluster-wide on certificate expiry and on a `Certificate` that never
goes Ready. To also alert on end-to-end HTTP/TLS reachability, such as a broken
IngressRoute, certificate or DNS record while replicas stay ready, add
{% if enable_public_ingress -%}
`{{ app_slug }}.{{ external_domain }}` to the cluster's static blackbox target
list ({% if enable_sso %}`module: http_sso` for this SSO-gated route{% else %}`module: http_2xx`{% endif %}).
{%- if enable_internal_ingress %}
Add `{{ app_slug }}.{{ internal_domain }}` with `module: http_2xx` too. It
carries its own IngressRoute, its own certificate and the DNS record from O5,
and the external probe covers none of them.
{%- endif %}
{%- else -%}
`{{ app_slug }}.{{ internal_domain }}` to the cluster's static blackbox target
list with `module: http_2xx`.
{%- endif %}

### O8 — SSO objects

{% if enable_sso -%}
This repo's route references the forward-auth middleware, which does nothing on
its own. In the cluster's Authentik Terraform, add: a **proxy** provider whose
`external_host` is EXACTLY `https://{{ app_slug }}.{{ external_domain }}`, the
application bound to it, the access group, the policy binding, and the provider
on the embedded outpost's list. Then plan, review and apply supervised.

Two failure modes worth knowing first: a provider missing from the outpost's
list answers every request with a 404 from the outpost, and an `external_host`
set to the other split-horizon hostname breaks the redirect loop rather than
erroring.
{%- if enable_internal_ingress %}

The middleware is on the public route only. `{{ app_slug }}.{{ internal_domain }}`
stays source-filtered to the LAN and tailnet rather than authenticated, because
a proxy provider pins one `external_host`. Gating it too means a second provider
and a second outpost entry.
{%- endif %}
{%- else -%}
Not needed: SSO is off for this tenant and its route carries no forward-auth
middleware. `copier update --data enable_sso=true` renders the middleware
reference and brings this step back.
{%- endif %}

### O9 — Branch → {{ change_request }} → merge

Flux reconciles the new tenant on the next cycle.

### O10 — Read-only kubeconfig

The wiring's `{{ app_slug }}-reader` ServiceAccount reads the namespace and the
tenant's own Flux objects. It cannot read Secrets or force a reconcile. Build
the tenant's kubeconfig from the cluster CA and a token for it. The subshell
stops at the first failure, so an empty CA or token never reaches the file:

```bash
(
  set -eu
  kubectl -n kube-system get configmap kube-root-ca.crt \
    -o jsonpath='{.data.ca\.crt}' > {{ app_slug }}-ca.crt
  test -s {{ app_slug }}-ca.crt || { echo 'no cluster CA read' >&2; exit 1; }
  TOKEN=$(kubectl -n {{ app_namespace }} create token {{ app_slug }}-reader --duration=2160h)
  test -n "$TOKEN" || { echo 'token creation failed' >&2; exit 1; }
  KC={{ app_slug }}.kubeconfig
  kubectl --kubeconfig "$KC" config set-cluster <cluster> \
    --server=https://<API_SERVER>:6443 \
    --certificate-authority={{ app_slug }}-ca.crt --embed-certs
  kubectl --kubeconfig "$KC" config set-credentials {{ app_slug }}-reader --token="$TOKEN"
  kubectl --kubeconfig "$KC" config set-context {{ app_slug }} \
    --cluster=<cluster> --user={{ app_slug }}-reader --namespace={{ app_namespace }}
  kubectl --kubeconfig "$KC" config use-context {{ app_slug }}
  kubectl --kubeconfig "$KC" auth can-i list pods -n {{ app_namespace }}
)
```

Hand the file over only when the last line prints `yes`, and through a channel
you would trust with a password. The reader sees the namespace's ConfigMaps and
pod logs, so keep secrets out of both.

The token expires. Rerun the block to issue a new one. The API server may cap
its lifetime below the duration asked for.

The API server address is reachable only from the cluster's own network. A
tenant off that network reaches it through the cluster's VPN, such as a
Tailscale subnet route or a WireGuard peer, so grant that access with the
kubeconfig. Never publish the API server for a tenant.

### Removal

Teardown is deliberate, and the namespace outlives the wiring. It takes one
step per change. The app Kustomization prunes as the wiring's
`{{ app_slug }}-flux` ServiceAccount. Removed in the same change, that
ServiceAccount and its RoleBindings can go first, and the prune then fails. The
workload, its routes and its certificates stay, and the grant keeps admitting
them.

1. Remove only the `{{ app_slug }}` app Kustomization from the wiring file.
   Wait until `flux get kustomizations -n flux-system` no longer lists it.
   Pruning the app Kustomization removes everything under `kubernetes/flux`.
{%- if database == 'postgres' and secrets_backend == 'onepassword' %}
2. Remove the `{{ app_slug }}-db` Kustomization the same way. It leaves the
   database running in place, and the `kubernetes/db` objects stay until you
   delete them by hand.
3. `git rm` the rest of the file and its `kustomization.yaml` line.
4. Confirm the last base backup and WAL archive in
   `{{ db_backup_bucket }}`, then delete the CNPG `Cluster` by hand.
{%- else %}
2. `git rm` the rest of the file and its `kustomization.yaml` line.
{%- endif %}

The `git rm` change prunes the GitRepository{% if secrets_backend != 'none' %}, the
ClusterSecretStore{% endif %}, the ServiceAccounts, the reader Role and the
RoleBindings.

Flux does not prune the Namespace, its ResourceQuota and LimitRange, the
`tenant-routing-{{ app_namespace }}` grant or its two bindings. A pruned
Namespace would cascade-delete every object and PVC in it, and that loss is
what the guard prevents.
{%- if database == 'postgres' and secrets_backend == 'onepassword' %}
`deletionPolicy: Orphan` on the database Kustomization cannot stop that
cascade.
{%- endif %}

Delete the Namespace by hand once nothing in it is wanted. While any wiring
still declares it, Flux recreates it. Then delete the grant ConfigMap, both
bindings and the `{{ app_slug }}-git-auth` Secret in `flux-system`. The
unmanaged-secrets gate reads the live cluster, so drop this tenant's
`ALLOWLIST` entries in a final change, once those Secrets are gone.
Revoke the git credential{% if secrets_backend == 'onepassword' %} and the tenant's Connect token, revoke
the `{{ onepassword_vault }}` grant with `op connect vault revoke`, and delete
that vault once its items are no longer needed{% elif secrets_backend == 'gitlab' %} and the tenant's project access
token{% endif %}.
